<BaseLayout title="How to learn English: a simple plan | LearnNest" description="Learn English step by step: core words, simple grammar, daily listening and speaking. A practical plan for beginners." path="/guides/how-to-learn-english" faqs={faqs}>
  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › How to learn English</p>
  <h1>How to learn English: a simple plan</h1>
  <p class="lede">English is the most useful language to learn, and the plan is simple: learn the most common words first, then simple grammar, then listen and speak a little every day. You do not need perfect grammar to start speaking — you need a few hundred words and the courage to use them.</p>

  <h2>Step 1 — Learn the 500 most common words</h2>
  <p>A small number of English words do most of the work. Words like time, people, way, day, man, thing, woman, life, child and world appear everywhere. Learn the most common nouns, verbs and adjectives first — with their meanings in your own language — and you will understand a surprising amount of everyday English.</p>
  <p>Learn words in phrases, not alone. Instead of just “make”, learn “make a mistake”, “make a decision”, “make breakfast”. English is full of these word partnerships, and learning them together makes you sound natural much sooner.</p>
  <h2>Step 2 — Learn simple grammar: present and past</h2>
  <p>Start with two tenses: the present simple for habits and facts (“I work every day”) and the past simple for finished actions (“I worked yesterday”). These two tenses cover most everyday conversation. Add the future with “going to” and “will” when you are comfortable.</p>
  <p>Do not try to learn every tense before you speak. Grammar is a tool, not a test — learn a little, use it in real sentences, then learn the next piece.</p>
  <h2>Step 3 — Listen every day</h2>
  <p>English pronunciation is the hardest part of the language, because spelling and sounds often do not match. The only cure is listening: podcasts for learners, TV shows with subtitles, songs, YouTube. Listen to the same short clip several times — first for the main idea, then for the words, then repeat the sentences aloud.</p>
  <p>Choose material that is slightly hard, not impossible. If you understand about 70–80% of it, that is the right level.</p>
  <h2>Step 4 — Speak every day, even a little</h2>
  <p>Speak from the start, even if it is just five minutes: describe your day, talk about the weather, or read a short text aloud. Speaking is a physical skill — your mouth needs practice forming English sounds, just like your brain needs practice choosing words.</p>
  <p>Mistakes are not failure; they are how you learn. A tutor helps most here, because they correct the mistakes you cannot hear yourself and keep you talking even when you get stuck.</p>
  <h2>Step 5 — Read and write a little every day</h2>
  <p>Read short, easy texts: graded readers, news for learners, or simple stories. Write a few sentences a day — a diary, a message, a summary of something you read. Writing forces you to build sentences carefully, which makes your speaking more accurate too.</p>
  <p>Keep a notebook of words and sentences you want to remember, and review it often. The learners who improve fastest are usually the ones who review, not the ones who study the most new material.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Studying only grammar and never speaking — English is a skill, not a subject.</li>
    <li>Trying to translate everything word for word from your own language.</li>
    <li>Learning rare, difficult words before the common ones you need every day.</li>
    <li>Being afraid of mistakes — every English learner makes them, including advanced ones.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Learn the most common words first — they give the fastest progress.</li>
    <li>Listen every day, even 10 minutes, and repeat what you hear.</li>
    <li>Speak a little every day, even to yourself.</li>
    <li>Review old words every week; forgetting is normal, reviewing fixes it.</li>
  </ul>
