  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How long does it take to learn English?</summary><p>It depends on your starting level and how much you study. A beginner studying 30 minutes a day can usually handle simple everyday conversations in 6–12 months. Reaching a comfortable, fluent level for work or study usually takes a few years — but you can use English usefully long before that.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is English hard to learn?</summary><p>English has easy parts: no noun gender, simple verb endings, and a huge amount of learning material. It has hard parts too: spelling does not match sounds, there are many irregular verbs, and phrasal verbs like “give up” or “look after” take time. Most learners find the start easy and the middle — getting from basic to fluent — the hardest part.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Should I learn British or American English?</summary><p>Either is fine, and they are mutually understandable. The differences are mostly spelling (colour/color), a few words (flat/apartment), and accent. Pick the one that matches your goal — work, study, travel — and stay consistent while you are learning.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Can I learn English by myself?</summary><p>Yes, for vocabulary, grammar, reading and listening. Speaking is the exception: you need a real person to practise with and to correct you. That is the part a tutor is for.</p></details>
